      {
        "id": 70181,
        "name": "Silverleaf",
        "username": "Silverleaf",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/silverleaf/{size}/2332_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-08-11T18:50:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>You see, we have a trade-off here. One one hand, icons or similar will make things less accessible to brand new players*, especially the ones who haven't read the rules**, and will require more effort when learning the game. And on the other, icons will make things easier/quicker/more accurate for new and experienced players (but probably won't make much difference to expert ones) <strong><em>for the whole of the rest of their Sentinels gaming experience</em></strong>.</p><p>Somewhat off-topic, but bear with me. I sing in a choir (actually I sing in 4, but I'm thinking of one in particular) where we work exclusively from sheet music and are expected to be able to accurately sing pieces we've never seen before, requiring us to have previously learned to read standard music notation (which does take some effort to learn). One of our members cannot, which makes things extremely difficult for him. It's only because he has the support of fellow section members who read well and because he has a good ear and a great slug of natural talent and can \"anticipate\" what will happen next that he manages to cope at all. The moral? Sometimes it's better to break through the barrier of some initial learning to make your experience easier or better later on.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>* I think we'd be underestimating the intelligence of new players to assume that they can't handle a few symbols easily. Put them on the back of the rule book in the appropriate place in the turn order sequence. I doubt very much that anyone would have to look them up after the intial learning-how-the-game-works stage (which maybe takes 10 minutes). We're not talking 7 Wonders here (a very easy game that's intially very hard to play because of the number of different icons).</p><p>** It's unreasonable to expect to be able to play a game like this without reading the rules or having someone who knows them already explain them. Therefore I'm inclined to discount such people.</p>",
