      {
        "id": 70182,
        "name": "Silverleaf",
        "username": "Silverleaf",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/silverleaf/{size}/2332_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-08-11T19:15:38.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Spiff\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/spiff/48/1865_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Spiff:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<blockquote>\nIcons reduce novice gamers interest in the game\n</blockquote>\nThis has been repeated a couple of times as if it's fact, but it's unsubtantiated as far as I know. &nbsp;Excessive use of overly complicated icons almost certainly would. &nbsp;There's no evidence that limited use of simple icons would.\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Not evidence here, but personal opinion - icons would chop the wall of text that is on some cards (by necessity at the moment) into smaller chunks with more white space to visually separate them. Kind of like bullet points, which are well known for making information easier to read and learn.</p>\n<p>I'd be very happy to use my (mostly very much novice) players as guinea pigs for testing purposes, if there's some way we can do this without playtesting material. And I'm probably useful too, fitting into the experienced camp.</p>",
        "post_number": 73,
