      {
        "id": 70185,
        "name": "Silverleaf",
        "username": "Silverleaf",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/silverleaf/{size}/2332_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-08-11T23:42:29.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Spiff\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/spiff/48/1865_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Spiff:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<blockquote>\nYou can definitely feel free to gather and share any data you wish, and we'll definitely take a look!\n</blockquote>\n... I've replicated the entire deck so that people can just print it out and play with it to see how the icons affect their experience...\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Brilliant! They look fantastic, great job.</p>\n<p>I've never actually fought Voss in a face-to-face actually-using-the-cards kind of way (although I've played against him in quite a few online games with Ronway dealing with all the SoT/EoT stuff). So that's perfect, I can go in with pretty much no knowledge of the card text and do a few tests with these and the official deck. Might even get some of my players to run the villain deck instead of me and see how that goes.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Spiff\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/spiff/48/1865_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Spiff:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Please don't get hung up on the fact that I used red and green colors for the icons. &nbsp;These aren't any kind of a finished product, and I know they're troublesome for some colorblind people. &nbsp;If it turned out that everyone loved the icons so much that &gt;G reprinted every single deck with icons tomorrow, I'd assume that they'd choose a more appropriate color.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I know I've mentioned it before, but there's no need to avoid red and green if you make sure that it's not the only way to distinguish between the two objects - a different background pattern, outline colour, size, letters in this case, or font can make even a red and green with similar values easily distinguishable by people with red-green colourblindness. Of course, sufficient contrast between the colours would be best. I've made my own copy of Qwirkle (notoriously bad for two of the common types of colourblindness) because my friend is mildly affected and wouldn't be able to distinguish some of the colours. I used red, orange, yellow, green, blue and a sort of mauve purple, and every hue was carefully selected to be accessible to any type of colourblindess (or even complete lack of colour vision - i do know someone who sees entirely in shades of grey). It's totally do-able.</p>",
        "post_number": 76,
        "post_type": 1,
